scenes like this were common in myanmar  until wednesday  crowds of people used to wait for hours  to get oxygen for a loved one  suffering from covet 19.  people are worried and scared there's  also been pressure from the military  telling people not to queue for oxygen  the junta has now imposed a new rule  people will need a letter from their  local administrator to obtain  oxygen like much of southeast asia  myanmar is experiencing its worst wave  of coronavirus infections  its healthcare system ravaged by decades  of military dictatorship  is struggling to cope the coup in  january  and the subsequent crackdown on medical  professionals for being one of the first  groups to go on strike is turning an  already dangerous health crisis  more deadly the military are closing  down charity clinics and arresting  doctors who volunteer there  they pretend to be patients and arrest  doctors that's why doctors are on the  run and in hiding  the junta has denied carrying out such  operations  this doctor who wants to keep his  identity secret  says he and his colleagues have had to  resort to using their phones to give  consultations  he estimates at least 70 percent of his  patients have tested positive for  coronavirus  hospitals are not accepting patients  i've heard of soldiers pointing their  guns at patients and telling them to go  home  they should not be the ones deciding who  gets admitted to hospital  some patients are also reluctant to go  to government health centers  citing a distrust of military-run  facilities  many end up self-isolating at home in  yangon  residents hang out yellow flags to show  they need medical supplies  hoping volunteers will help the number  of people  dying in their homes is increasing  ambulance services normally take  patients to hospitals but these days  there are more requests to pick up  bodies  funeral services are overloaded some  families have had to wait several days  before a body can be transported  as coronavirus infections and deaths  soar  the un independent expert on human  rights in myanmar tom andrews  has called for a covert ceasefire urging  the international community to pressure  the junta to stop its attacks on  healthcare workers  so they can do what they are meant to do  deliver medical care  florence louis al jazeera
 

